+# Quarterly operations report — Q2 2026 (May–July)
+
+## Summary
+
+The quarter opened strong, with revenue climbing from €412,380 in May to €431,205
+in June (+4.6%), before pulling back to €389,640 in July (-9.6%), which was also
+below the May starting point. Costs held steady in the €151K–€158K range
+throughout, so the July revenue dip flowed straight through to profitability:
+the quarter still closed with a healthy €765,585 net and a 62.1% margin, but
+that comfortable cushion was built mostly in May and June. Customer growth
+followed a similar downward trend — new signings and net adds shrank every
+month even as churn ticked up — while the support team kept pace with demand,
+closing 95.5% of tickets opened across the quarter.
+
+## Key figures
+
+| Metric | Value |
+|---|---|
+| Quarterly revenue | €1,233,225 |
+| Quarterly costs | €467,640 |
+| Net | €765,585 |
+| Margin | 62.1% |
+| Net customer adds | 153 |
+| Ticket close rate | 95.5% |
+| June revenue growth | 4.6% |
+| July revenue growth | -9.6% |
+
+## Notes
+
+Net customer adds fell every month of the quarter — 69 in May, 52 in June,
+32 in July — driven by a double squeeze of fewer new signings (86 → 74 → 63)
+and rising churn (17 → 22 → 31). Worth watching whether this is a one-quarter
+blip or the start of a trend, especially alongside the July revenue decline.
+On the support side, ticket volume dropped alongside the customer trends
+(342 → 361 → 298 opened), but the close rate actually improved through the
+quarter (92.1% in May to 97.7% in July), so service quality isn't a concern.
+Costs were essentially flat all quarter, which is good discipline, but it
+also means margin is now fully exposed to top-line swings — there's no cost
+buffer absorbing the July revenue miss.
